The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (eeh_has_flag(EEH_PROBE_MODE_DEV))
    eeh_ops->probe(pdn, NULL);
```
Reason:
The probe call is executed before checking for and handling outstanding krefs. If a device has an existing PE (Processing Element), the probe operation will exit without rediscovering the device, leading to issues with devices that have outstanding krefs not being properly handled after a complete hotplug.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
/*
 * The EEH cache might not be removed correctly because of
 * unbalanced kref to the device during unplug time, which
 * relies on pcibios_release_device(). So we have to remove
 * that here explicitly.
 */
if (edev->pdev) {
    eeh_rmv_from_parent_pe(edev);
    eeh_addr_cache_rmv_dev(edev->pdev);
    eeh_sysfs_remove_device(edev->pdev);
    edev->mode &= ~EEH_DEV_SYSFS;

    edev->mode |= EEH_DEV_NO_HANDLER;

    edev->pdev = NULL;
    dev->dev.archdata.edev = NULL;
}

if (eeh_has_flag(EEH_PROBE_MODE_DEV))
    eeh_ops->probe(pdn, NULL);
```
Reason:
The patch moves the probe call to after the kref check and handling of the existing PE. This ensures that if there are outstanding krefs, the PE is properly destroyed before attempting to probe the device, allowing for correct rediscovery of devices after a complete hotplug.

